  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and scattered thunderstorms are expected \nahead of a cold front through tonight. A few of these storms may \nbecome severe with damaging wind gusts being the primary hazard.\n\nAn unseasonably deep low for early September will move across Hudson \nBay and into northern Quebec through tonight. An associated cold \nfront, currently located across the Upper Great Lakes region will \napproach the region today before crossing tonight. Widespread dry \nweather across the eastern Great Lakes region will end this morning. \nIsentropic lift and increasing moisture will bring light rain \nshowers through daybreak, with the highest chance along and north of \nInterstate-90. \n\nThe mid-level pattern becomes zonal today as a plume of deep \nmoisture enters the eastern Great Lakes region. Daytime heating and \nincreasing southerly flow will lead to surface based instability \nbetween 750-1500 J/kg across western NY, with the higher end \nacross the western Southern Tier where less cloud cover is \nexpected. Cloud cover and showers will likely limit\ndestabilization across the eastern Lake Ontario region.\n\nA 40-knot 850mb jet will move across the forecast area today with \nthe core of the strongest winds expected to extend from Lake Erie to \nthe Saint Lawrence Valley. Breezy conditions are expected northeast \nof the Lakes today. Wind gusts of 30-35 mph are expected across the \nNiagara Frontier and the Saint Lawrence Valley. The strengthening \nlow to mid level wind profile today will lead to 0-6km shear around \n40 knots this afternoon through this evening. Forecast surface \nanalysis depicts a pre-frontal trough will cross the region this \nafternoon through evening. Low level convergence coupled with \ninstability and shear will support thunderstorms with the potential \nof isolated to scattered severe thunderstorms possible across \nwestern and north-central NY. The higher probability lies across the \nwestern Southern Tier this evening, where the best chance for \ndestabilization will occur. The primary threat remains damaging \nwinds, however due to increasing storm relative helicity when SBCAPE \nis the highest, an isolated tornado can't be ruled out. \n\nHeavy downpours are expected in thunderstorms through this evening \nas a plume of forecast PWATS of 1.8 inches (99th percentile) crosses \nthe region. Storms are expected to be progressive, however due to \nhigh PWATS and training along the frontal boundary there remains a \nlow risk of flooding across the western Southern Tier to eastern \nLake Ontario region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A few showers may linger Thursday mainly across the \nSouthern Tier, with dry weather returning Friday through Saturday.\n\nThe cold front will become orientated west to east south of the New \nYork State line Thursday. A few showers may linger across western NY \ninto Thursday morning. Cloud cover will slowly decrease from north \nto south Thursday with most areas rain-free. \n\nSurface high pressure will build into the region late Thursday \nthrough Thursday night and the cold front will move away from the \nforecast area. Dry weather is expected through the start of the \nweekend.",
